# Terra Game — Cursor Development Rules

## 1. PROJECT OVERVIEW
Unity 6 2D mobile single-player game on a completely fictional planet.
Start with a small territory, open the 2D world map, select connected territories,
attack through a falling-block puzzle, complete one or multiple expeditions,
earn separate Gold and Score, spend Hearts to enter battles, use Gold in the Shop,
spin the Daily Wheel, complete Missions and Achievements, and view an online global leaderboard.
The game is NOT multiplayer. Gameplay must remain playable offline.
Online services are an additional layer and must never be tightly coupled to core gameplay.

## 2. ENGINE
Use Unity 6, C#, 2D, Unity Input System, TextMeshPro, ScriptableObjects for static data,
JSON local saves, and scene-based architecture. Default to Built-in Render Pipeline
unless explicitly requested otherwise. Explain pipeline migrations before changing settings.
Do not introduce URP into a Built-in project.
Do not introduce DOTS, ECS, Addressables, large systems or third-party packages without request.

## 3. CORE ARCHITECTURE
Prefer modular systems, not giant managers. Responsibilities belong to GameBootstrap,
GameManager, GameStateManager, SceneLoader, AudioManager, SaveManager, EconomyManager,
ExpeditionManager, BattleManager, PuzzleGameManager, MissionManager, AchievementManager,
DailyWheelManager, ShopManager, LeaderboardManager, and UIManager where needed.
Before adding a manager: search the project, identify related functionality, reuse/extend
the existing owner, and only add a system for a genuinely different responsibility.
Never duplicate managers (EconomyManager2, NewGameManager, BattleManagerNew, SaveManagerV2).

## 4. SINGLE RESPONSIBILITY
Economy owns Gold, Hearts, Max Hearts and Attack Speed, not UI, navigation, puzzle, audio or missions.
PuzzleGrid owns cells, occupancy and placement; clearing is delegated to its dedicated system.
UI displays data and sends actions. Core game rules never belong in UI classes.

## 5. SCRIPTABLEOBJECT RULES
ScriptableObjects contain immutable configuration, never mutable player progress.
Examples: RegionData, ContinentData, PuzzlePieceData, PuzzleLevelData, PuzzleDifficultyData,
CampaignData, ShopProductData, WheelSegmentData, MissionData, AchievementData, BalanceConfiguration.
Never assign RegionData.isCaptured. Runtime ownership belongs to WorldProgressData/save models.

## 6. DATA-DRIVEN DESIGN
Rewards, heart costs, targets, time limits, fall speed, difficulty, campaign counts,
prices, upgrades, combos and merge values belong in configuration.
Use RewardService.ApplyReward; avoid unrelated gold += 50 and magic gameplay numbers.

## 7. WORLD MAP
Entirely fictional continents, regions, biomes, rivers, mountains, lakes, seas, islands,
volcanic, crystal and mystery areas. Biomes: Grassland, Forest, Desert, Ice, Volcanic,
Crystal, Toxic, Ocean/Island. States: Locked, Available, Attacking, PartiallyCaptured, Captured.
Static information and runtime ownership must remain separate.

## 8. REGION SYSTEM
Regions require configurable 1, 2, 5, 10 or future larger expedition counts.
Success increments campaign progress; failure never does. Capture when progress >= required.
Only configured nextRegionIds unlock; never unlock unrelated regions automatically.

## 9. BATTLE FLOW
WORLD MAP -> REGION SELECT -> ATTACK -> BATTLE PREPARATION -> EXPEDITION -> PUZZLE
-> SUCCESS/FAILURE -> CAMPAIGN RESULT -> REGION PROGRESS -> WORLD MAP.
BattleSessionData transports regionId, campaignIndex, difficulty, targetScore and timeLimit.
No static global battle configuration. Do not bypass flow without an explicit reason.

## 10. HEART SYSTEM
Start with 5 Hearts and 5 Max Hearts; entry normally costs 1 Heart.
CanPlay/ConsumeHeart belong to EconomyManager. UI never changes heart values directly.
No refund after failure unless design changes explicitly.

## 11. GOLD SYSTEM
Gold comes from battles, captures, wheel, missions and achievements.
Spend on hearts, capacity and attack upgrades. Gold cannot go negative.
Every change passes through EconomyManager/RewardService; no player.gold += from UI.

## 12. REWARD SYSTEM
RewardService applies Gold, Hearts, Attack Speed, Max Hearts and future rewards centrally.
Use unique transaction/result IDs for duplicate protection. Battle results, captures,
wheel rewards, mission claims and achievement claims must never pay twice.
Persist progress and reward transaction together; roll back failed writes.

## 13. PUZZLE SYSTEM
Falling-block inspiration, not necessarily classic Tetris. Prototype: configurable 8x12,
approximately 90 seconds and approximately 1000 target points.
Shapes: Single, Horizontal 2/3, Vertical 2/3, Square 2x2, L, Reverse L, T, Z, Reverse Z.
Separate PuzzleGrid, PuzzlePiece, PieceSpawner, PieceController, GhostPieceController,
MergeSystem, LineClearSystem, ScoreSystem, ComboSystem, BattleTimer and ObjectiveSystem.

## 14. MERGE SYSTEM
Same-type/same-level blocks may combine: 1+1=2, 2+2=3, 3+3=4.
Progression is configurable and lives in MergeSystem, not PuzzleGrid.
Merges can produce combo chains, score bonuses, visuals, audio and haptics.

## 15. DIFFICULTY
Easy, Normal, Hard, VeryHard, Extreme; data-driven through one central resolver.
May affect fall speed, time, target, spawn rate, variety, board conditions,
special rules and score multiplier. Avoid sudden spikes and scattered calculations.

## 16. PLAYER ATTACK SPEED
Default 1.0, upgradeable, bounded through configuration. Its established puzzle/battle
effect must remain explicit and must not trivialize late-game difficulty.

## 17. SHOP
Gold-only purchases; no real-money IAP unless requested.
Initial products: +1 Heart, Heart Pack, Max Heart Upgrade, Attack Speed Upgrade.
Static products belong in ShopProductData or central static configuration.
Purchase levels belong to ShopProgressData, never ScriptableObjects.

## 18. DAILY WHEEL
Check eligibility, determine reward centrally before animation, animate to exactly that
segment, apply through RewardService, save transaction, then display result.
Use central RNG and TimeService. Do not scatter DateTime.Now. Interrupted spins must recover.

## 19. MISSIONS
Event-driven via BattleCompleted, BattleWon, RegionCaptured, GoldEarned, GoldSpent,
MergePerformed, LineCleared, ComboReached, DailySpinCompleted and ShopPurchaseCompleted.
MissionManager listens; never distribute mission-specific if statements throughout gameplay.

## 20. ACHIEVEMENTS
Permanent saved progression: first victory, 10 captures, 10k/100k score, 100 merges,
10x combo, Hard victory and 10 spins. Rewards use RewardService; claims are duplicate-safe.

## 21. SCORE
Score powers progression, leaderboard, achievements and records; Gold powers economy.
They are distinct. Battle scoring belongs to ScoreSystem, never direct UI mutation.

## 22. ONLINE ARCHITECTURE
Gameplay remains offline-capable. Use IPlayerService, ILeaderboardService, ICloudSaveService.
Development can use MockPlayerService and MockLeaderboardService.
Never couple gameplay to a backend. Support future Game Center, Play Games or custom providers.

## 23. LEADERBOARD
Global Top 100 plus player's own rank, name, score and captured regions.
Show own row even outside Top 100. Leaderboard must never block offline gameplay.
Mock data must be visibly labeled and never presented as real global players.

## 24. SAVE SYSTEM
Versioned JSON with PlayerData, WorldProgress, EconomyData, ShopProgressData,
DailyWheelProgressData, MissionProgressData, AchievementProgressData and SettingsData.
Validate data, handle missing fields, old versions, corruption, migration and duplicate rewards.
Never destroy saves because fields are missing; preserve incompatible/corrupt originals.

## 25. UI RULES
Mobile-first, touch-friendly, responsive, safe-area aware, TextMeshPro.
Canvas Scaler uses Scale With Screen Size. Respect notch, Dynamic Island, home indicator
and aspect ratios. Large touch areas; state must never rely only on color.

## 26. INPUT
Use Unity Input System and shared input abstraction for mouse and touch.
Map: one-finger pan, two-finger pinch, tap selection. Battle: drag/swipe movement,
rotation button, optional downward swipe/drop. UI takes priority over gameplay.

## 27. INPUT PRIORITY
Modal Popup > UI > Gameplay > World Map. Block gameplay under modal popups.
UI gestures must not leak to gameplay/map. Guard double starts, purchases, spins and claims.

## 28. SCENE ARCHITECTURE
Boot, MainMenu, WorldMap, Battle, Shop, DailyWheel, Leaderboard.
SceneLoader owns transitions. Avoid unnecessary scenes and tight inter-scene coupling.

## 29. AUDIO
AudioManager owns Music, SFX, UI and Ambient cues; respect musicEnabled/soundEnabled.
Do not play clips directly from many unrelated scripts.

## 30. VFX AND GAME FEEL
Modular event-driven feedback: unlock sparkle, selection glow, capture animation,
placement, merge, combo, line clear, score popup, timer warning, victory and rewards.
Keep VFX implementation outside core rules.

## 31. PERFORMANCE
Mobile constraints: pooling, reusable UI, events, few Update loops, cached references,
efficient collections, small textures and limited particles. Avoid per-frame instantiate/destroy,
scene searches, hot-loop LINQ, large allocations and unnecessary Canvas rebuilds.
Optimize where useful without sacrificing maintainability.

## 32. NULL SAFETY
Inspect existing references, serialized fields, prefabs, scenes and dependencies before changes.
Never silently replace references. Validate required references and report actionable errors.

## 33. EVENT SUBSCRIPTIONS
Each subscription needs an unsubscribe path (OnEnable/OnDisable or explicit disposal).
Avoid duplicates during scene transitions, UI toggles, battle restarts, save/load,
wheel spins, missions and leaderboard changes.

## 34. DEBUG TOOLS
Optional add currency/hearts, unlock/capture/reset, set score/difficulty/attack,
skip campaign and complete mission tools must be development-only and absent from production.

## 35. CODE STYLE
Clear names, private/serialized fields, interfaces for replaceable services, enums,
small methods, meaningful comments and consistent namespaces. Avoid giant classes,
cryptic names, duplication, magic strings/numbers and excessive comments.

## 36. BEFORE EDITING ANY FILE
Read relevant code, search usages, identify dependencies, make the smallest safe change,
preserve functionality, check compilation and related scene/prefab/data references.
Do not rewrite a system when a small change suffices.

## 37. NEVER BREAK EXISTING SYSTEMS
Do not delete working functionality, replace architecture unnecessarily, rename unchecked
public APIs, remove serialized fields without migration, change saves without migration,
or remove scene objects without reference checks. Explain necessary refactors; preserve compatibility.

## 38. TESTING
Check compilation, null safety, references, save/load, UI/input/mobile and regressions.
Verify Start -> Map -> Region -> Attack -> Spend Heart -> Puzzle -> Win -> Reward
-> Campaign -> Capture -> Unlock -> Save -> Reload. Also verify failure paths.

## 39. MOBILE FIRST
Consider iPhone, Android, portrait/aspect-ratio layouts, safe areas, touch, performance,
memory, battery, app pause/resume and foreground/background. Never assume desktop-only input.

## 40. FUTURE EXTENSIBILITY
Allow more regions/continents/biomes/pieces, boss/treasure/mystery/story regions,
shop items, missions, seasons, daily challenges, backends, cloud save, Game Center,
Play Games, localization and platforms. Do not implement future features without request.

## 41. SPECIAL PUZZLE PIECES
Future Bomb, Lightning, Freeze, Wild, Multiplier, Golden, Time Bonus, Score Bonus,
Shield, Hammer, Undo and Extra Move must use extensible data-driven systems.
Never hardcode each into PuzzleGameManager.

## 42. ERROR HANDLING
Clear, actionable, contextual errors, e.g. BattleManager: BattleSessionData is missing.
Never silently swallow important exceptions or spam the console every frame.

## 43. LOGGING
Useful development logs such as expedition 2/5 completed; no noisy Update logs.
Production logging is limited.

## 44. SECURITY / ANTI-CHEAT READINESS
Client data is untrusted for online services. Future leaderboard results are server-authoritative.
Structured submission: submissionId, playerId, score, regionId, campaignIndex,
difficulty, duration, timestamp. Never expose arbitrary leaderboard score assignment.
Do not add actual backend/anti-cheat without request.

## 45. DEVELOPMENT PROCESS
Inspect architecture, identify affected systems, reuse owners, change necessary files,
keep modules small, compile, verify references, test relevant flow and report exact changes.
Do not blindly generate dozens of files.

## 46. AGENT BEHAVIOR
Inspect first, preserve working functionality, validate references, report changed files,
known limitations and compile/test status. Do not invent existing classes or files,
duplicate systems, rewrite unrelated code, remove functionality without authorization,
add unnecessary packages/backends/IAP/ads or silently change core design.

## 47. GAME DESIGN PRIORITY
Existing working gameplay > established architecture > player experience > mobile usability
> performance > extensibility > implementation convenience. Do not change rules for convenience.

## 48. CURRENT PRODUCT VISION
"Explore a cute fictional planet, expand your territory, and conquer new lands through
satisfying falling-block puzzle battles."
Cute, colorful, clean, modern, friendly, slightly sci-fi, lighthearted, polished mobile casual.
Avoid dark military realism, complex/generic fantasy UI, aggressive VFX and clutter.
Product name: TerraTactix. Reference layout: main menu, large world map and block-battle screen.
Keep many territories and configurable multiple expeditions for a long campaign.

## 49. IMPORTANT
Explicit user changes override defaults. Before major architecture changes explain what,
why, affected systems, save compatibility and scene/prefab changes. Never rewrite silently.
Do not treat unconfirmed assistant proposals as user approvals.
